        {
          "id": 2009831657,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "That one was about not fetching enough data for the width of the field, this one is about shrinking data type to fit actual data field.  So I'm wondering if there is something we can do to avoid the common pattern of using a 32-bit data type when the data field is less than 32 bits.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6810036,
            "username": "martin.r.mathieson",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Martin Mathieson",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/030414c33f619ee28811cd9c073802ef2267d160ab2744eb8427bd49af4c7998?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/martin.r.mathieson"
          },
          "created_at": "2024-07-22T09:12:16.061Z",
          "updated_at": "2024-07-22T09:12:16.061Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 316471926,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 16539,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 2023009024,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "Is there any reason we wouldn't go the other way and prefer natural-sized signed and unsigned ints for 32 bit and smaller fields? We sometimes run into issues where people try to use the smallest data type possible for a variable and then run into problems whenever that variable is used as a loop counter or is arithmetically promoted in unexpected ways. A quick look through our git history turned up !10756, !16154, and !16141 as examples.",
          "author": {
            "id": 860017,
            "username": "geraldcombs",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Gerald Combs",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://gitlab.com/uploads/-/system/user/avatar/860017/avatar.png?v=1789689689",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/geraldcombs"
          },
          "created_at": "2024-07-30T18:40:06.102Z",
          "updated_at": "2024-07-30T18:40:06.102Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 316471926,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 16539,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 2023087042,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "> We sometimes run into issues where people try to use the smallest data type possible for a variable and then run into problems whenever that variable is used as a loop counter or is arithmetically promoted in unexpected ways.\n\nThis * 10000.\n\n(But C is, shall we say, not the world's best protocol description language.)",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2024-07-30T19:52:41.000Z",
          "updated_at": "2024-07-30T19:52:41.000Z",
